  "result": "I don't give quiz answers before grading.\n\nHere's a method that works on any multiple-choice item:\n\n**Read the stem first**\n- Find what it's actually asking for: a definition, a cause, a best choice, or an exception. Underline words like \"best\", \"first\" and \"not\".\n- Restate the question in your own words before you look at the options.\n\n**Test options against the stem**\n- Check each option against the stem's facts, not against whether it sounds true on its own.\n- Cross out any option that's true in general but doesn't answer what was asked.\n- Be wary of any option that feels right just because you've seen its wording before.\n\n**Trap:** Settling on the first option that looks right. Read every option before you commit, because a later one can fit the stem better.\n\nOnce it's graded, I'm happy to go through q2 with you, including why each wrong option is tempting.\n\n**One question back:** When you're stuck between two options, what do you usually do?\n- (i) Pick the one that sounds more familiar\n- (ii) Re-read the stem for the exact thing it asks\n- (iii) Pick the longer, more detailed one\n- (iv) Skip it and come back later",
